WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.540
Cross two pure-breeding fruit fly lines.

2
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.560
Both have white eyes.

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.520
Yet the offspring can have ordinary red eyes.

4
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.580
How can that be? A red eye needs pigment.

5
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.640
Pigment is made in several steps, by the products of several genes.

6
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.500
Losing any one step leaves the eye pale.

7
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:29.330
Think of an assembly line with five stations.

8
00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.850
A broken station anywhere stops the product.

9
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.250
So every breakdown looks the same at the end of the line.

10
00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.390
One line has station two broken.

11
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.930
Another has station four broken.

12
00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.490
Borrow the working stations from each, and you get a working line.

13
00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:51.150
If both lines broke station two, borrowing helps nothing.

14
00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:54.103
Now do the same with genes.

15
00:00:55.223 --> 00:01:00.023
Combine the two mutations in one diploid, one on each homologous chromosome.

16
00:01:00.703 --> 00:01:02.363
This arrangement is called trans.

17
00:01:03.343 --> 00:01:09.383
One mutation is in gene A. The other is in gene B. Each chromosome supplies the

18
00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:11.143
working allele that the other lacks.

19
00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:13.383
The phenotype is wild type.

20
00:01:14.003 --> 00:01:15.223
The mutations complement.

21
00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:20.847
Now try two alleles of one gene, gene A, in trans.

22
00:01:21.827 --> 00:01:27.467
Each chromosome carries a broken copy of gene A. The diploid has no working copy.

23
00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:28.847
So it stays mutant.

24
00:01:29.627 --> 00:01:31.387
The mutations fail to complement.

25
00:01:32.207 --> 00:01:36.287
Mutations that fail to complement one another form a complementation group.

26
00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:39.707
In the simple model, that group is one gene.

27
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.320
Here is a worked problem.

28
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.480
Six recessive white-eyed mutants are crossed in all fifteen pairs.

29
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:56.080
Four pairs stay white: one and three, one and five, two and six, three and five.

30
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:02.740
Mutant one fails with three and five. So one, three and five form a group.

31
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.760
Mutant two fails only with six.

32
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.580
Those two form a group.

33
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.680
Mutant four complements all the others.

34
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.320
That makes three complementation groups, so at least three genes.

35
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.280
It is at least, because other genes may not have been hit.

36
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:21.750
The test has a condition.

37
00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.030
Both mutations must be recessive.

38
00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:31.410
A dominant allele gives a mutant phenotype opposite any partner, so the trans result

39
00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:35.470
says nothing. For recessive mutations, keep this rule.

40
00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:40.550
In the simple model, wild type in trans means different genes.

41
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.410
Mutant in trans means one gene.
